WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.960
What is this thing?

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.640
In fairness, when the first microscopes were
turned on samples of muddy water, that was

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:26.000
a common question.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:33.860
And the discoverers of that time were seeing
new things every day that puzzled and baffled

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:34.870
them.

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.399
But you would think that, by now, we’d have
a pretty good idea.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.600
And yes, this is a rotifer...but...what is
it?

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.840
There are around 2,000 species
of rotifers distributed in watery habitats

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.359
around the world.

00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:58.840
And so it’s no surprise that they’ve come
up on our channel quite frequently.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:03.940
Unfortunately, their situations on here are
usually quite dire.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.640
They’ve been captured.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.380
They’ve been swallowed alive.

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.460
Sometimes their body fluids are getting sucked
out of them.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:18.070
You may even remember the one rotifer trying
to lay its egg as it was slowly being eaten

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.680
by a heliozoan.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:30.420
So we need to cut the poor little rotifer
some slack, maybe even make a case for them.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.799
Even if, or maybe because, they seem to have
a hard time doing that on their own.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:44.440
Even if we, even now, don’t know precisely
what they even are.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:47.580
Rotifers are definitely an animal.

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:54.869
And they are a type of invertebrate, but beyond
that, here we are are in 2019 and, like, we

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:56.650
just really don’t know.

00:01:56.650 --> 00:02:02.310
They’re just really...different from other
animals, though we think they might be related

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:08.750
to arrow worms, a tiny animal that is a major
component of marine plankton, that’s something

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:12.190
we first uncovered literally last year.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:18.670
It’s been challenging to map out the evolution
and relationships of the Rotifera phylum because

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:25.900
despite their apparent propensity for dying,
their microscopic, soft bodies make for poor

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:27.070
fossils.

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:35.520
We homo sapiens have only found one single
rotifer fossil from outside of the Holocene,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:38.250
which is our current geological epoch.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:46.530
We have one ONE sample of a rotifer...fossilized
in Amber from around 35 million years ago,

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:55.500
during the Eocene, and it looks weirdly identical
to rotifers that still exist today.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:03:01.030
The vast bulk of the Rotifera phylum, make
their home in fresh water, and they can be

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:08.890
found around the world, from small puddles,
to sewage ponds, to crisp, Alpine lakes.

00:03:08.890 --> 00:03:14.940
They may be microscopic, their bodies typically
ranging from 100 to 500 microns long, but

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:22.380
their tiny bodies contain even tinier organs:
from a simple brain, a small stomach a kind

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:29.890
of jaw, a nervous system, all compartmentalized
in various regions of its body.

00:03:29.890 --> 00:03:34.090
Rotifers come in many different shapes, but
you can generally break their bodies down

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.310
into four regions.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:40.220
You’ve got the head, the neck, the body,
and the foot.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:46.370
At the top of the rotifer’s head is the
corona, a crown made up of cilia whose movement

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:53.450
resembles a turning wheel and that is what
gives the organism its name: in Latin, “rotifer”

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.190
translates to “wheel-bearer.”

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:01.360
The corona helps the rotifer eat, the beating
of the cilia creating a vortex that draws

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:04.820
food, like micro algae, into the mouth.

00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:10.790
Then the rotifer uses its jaw, called the
trophi, to grind down the food.

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:16.329
The hardness of the trophi makes it one of
the few parts of rotifers that we can find

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:22.330
fossils of, and along with the corona, it
is one of the unifying characteristics of

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:23.720
rotifers.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.080
Some rotifers reproduce strictly through parthenogenesis,

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.220
a form of asexual reproduction, while others

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:33.970
use both asexual and sexual reproduction.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:41.120
Either way, rotifers are eutelic, which means
that from the moment they are born, they will

00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:46.350
have a fixed number of cells in their bodies
for the rest of their life.

00:04:46.350 --> 00:04:53.060
For most rotifers, that number is around 1000
cells . And for the rotifer to grow, the cells

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:58.560
themselves—which cannot divide—have to
get bigger.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:04.100
About a quarter of the cells in a rotifer’s
body are dedicated to its nervous system,

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:09.400
which lets the rotifer process its surroundings
and respond accordingly.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:14.650
As you may have noticed, a lot of rotifer
species are quite stretchy, extending and

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:18.430
contracting as the organism navigates the
world.

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:23.370
At the end of the rotifer’s body, where
the foot lies, you might find glands that

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:30.551
secrete a sort of glue that the rotifer can
use to attach itself to a surface.

00:05:30.551 --> 00:05:35.740
Now, some rotifers form colonies, which can
be as small as two members, to some much larger

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.290
colonies that have more than 1,000.

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:43.310
These rotifer gatherings can form through
different recruitment methods.

00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:48.450
Sometimes a colony is already established,
and a young rotifer comes by and joins in.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:54.490
Other colonies are constantly developing and
expanding as young rotifers are born into

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:58.780
them, getting smaller only as the colony dies
or breaks up.

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:05.930
And sometimes, a bunch of young rotifers decide
to just trek out and establish their own colony

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:06.930
together.

00:06:06.930 --> 00:06:08.960
How do they decide to do that?

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:10.000
I dunno.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.060
They’re mysterious.

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:17.710
For all these different ways they start, rotifers
inside a colony don’t seem to share resources,

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:22.650
so it’s not quite clear what the colony’s
purpose is.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.550
Maybe it helps protect them from predators.

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:31.490
Or maybe it’s….something else...remember...mysterious.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:37.360
While rotifers can live in the thin films
of water found in more earthly environments,

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.220
their habitats can change.

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:45.050
Moss, lichen, or soil, where rotifers may
take up residence because of the dampness,

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:51.350
are still subject to the daily and seasonal
water cycles, and as evaporation dries out

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:58.650
their surroundings, many rotifers protect
themselves by entering a sort of resting state

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.600
called anhydrobiosis.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:07.820
We don’t have an image of this to show you,
but imagine this rotifer shedding water from

00:07:07.820 --> 00:07:14.000
its body until it’s about 30-40% of its
original weight and compacted into a little

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:21.690
ball, appearing from the outside like nothing
more than a tiny grain of silt.

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:27.050
But when water comes back to the rotifer,
it will switch out of this state and back

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:33.860
to an active one, waking up without having
aged like a microscopic Sleeping Beauty.

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:43.580
Even though they are microscopic and often
ignored by us macro-animals, and apparently

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:50.270
often bullied and consumed by their single-celled
neighbors, rotifers are charmingly bizarre

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:51.860
animals.

00:07:51.860 --> 00:07:58.020
They are everywhere, minding their own business,
living their own lives, and turning themselves

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:04.110
into inactive little balls when life gets
tough, which makes them, you know, not unlike

00:08:04.110 --> 00:08:05.900
us sometimes.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:11.419
So let’s end with a tribute to the rotifer,
giving it the dignity of words spoken to the

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:16.400
Royal Society in the 18th century by rotifer-observer,
Mr. Baker:

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:24.420
“I call it a Water Animal, because its appearance
as a living creature is only in that Element.

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:32.450
I give it also for Distinction Sake the Name
of Wheeler, Wheel Insect, or Animal;”

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:39.540
Wheel away little friend, whatever your lineage, however you came to exist. Wheel away.

00:08:42.140 --> 00:08:45.020
Thank you for coming on this journey with us

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:49.100
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.480
Thank you, especially, to all of our patrons.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:55.140
You can find us at patreon.com/journeytomicro

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.900
And if you want to see more from our Master of Microscopes, James

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:02.400
check out Jam and Germs on Instagram.

